Subject: Register VM: the argument staging index is built for the baseline tier too

What this does
  - quickjs.c js_regvm_encode: the staging index of call arguments (patch 0168, `aw_def` / `aw_reads`) is built whenever the function has values, baseline tier included. It was skipped for `regvm_baseline` functions.

Why we need it
  Without the index every call argument of a baseline-tier function falls back to `regvm_arg_retarget_scan`, a scan of the instructions already emitted, so a function with very many call arguments (mandreel's generated functions) takes time quadratic in its size: 350 s for the interpreter alone, 92% of it in that scan. With the index the same run takes 5.4 s and scores the same.

Soundness
  The index only answers the question the scan answers (the definition and the reads of a staged argument window); the baseline tier asks it the same way and gets the same answers. The index's arrays are sized by the function's value count.

Failure modes
  The two arrays use memory proportional to the value count of every baseline-tier function while it is encoded; they are freed with the encoder state as for the other tiers.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -79031,3 +79031,3 @@
-    /* The baseline tier has no SSA or argument retargeting, and the staging
-       index's arrays grow with the peak depth, so it is disabled there. */
-    if (n_values > 0 && !fd->regvm_baseline) {
+    /* The staging index answers the retarget scan's questions without
+       scanning, so the baseline tier needs it as much as the others. */
+    if (n_values > 0) {
